<Docs
  title="Running GitWarren on Windows — install, start at logon, WSL"
  description="Install GitWarren on Windows, have it start at logon, point an agent at a Windows-native repository, and reach a WSL distro as a separate host."
  eyebrow="Windows"
  heading="Windows-native, and first class."
  intro="Most Windows developers do not use WSL, and coding agents have run natively on Windows since late 2025. A repository on C: is an ordinary local repository — no host in front of it, and nothing about the Windows app made worse to accommodate the ones that are."
>
  <h2>Installing</h2>

  <p>
    One installer, from <a href="/#download">the front page</a>, carrying both
    x64 and arm64. It installs per user, which is what makes the whole thing
    quiet: no UAC prompt on install, and no UAC prompt when it updates itself in
    the background.
  </p>

  <p>
    The per-architecture <code>.exe</code> files sit beside it on the releases
    page. They are build artefacts; the universal installer is the one to hand
    anybody.
  </p>

  <h2>Starting at logon</h2>

  <p>
    The desktop app registers itself to start with Windows, and lives in the
    notification area so links from your terminal have something to open.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the command-line half — <code>npx gitwarren serve</code> is the way to
    get it on Windows — <code>gitwarren service install</code> registers an
    at-logon Scheduled Task named <code>GitWarren daemon</code> and runs it
    straight away. The name is one you would actually guess if you went looking
    through Task Scheduler for what keeps starting.
  </p>

  <pre><code>gitwarren service install
gitwarren service status
gitwarren service uninstall</code></pre>

  <h2>Pointing an agent at it</h2>

  <p>
    Paste this into whatever agent you run on the machine — Claude Code, Codex,
    or anything else speaking MCP:
  </p>

  <pre><code>Set up the GitWarren MCP server for yourself. It speaks MCP over
stdio and is started with the command ~/.gitwarren/bin/gitwarren-mcp
(no arguments, no environment). Register it under the name
"gitwarren" in your own MCP configuration, then call its
agent_identity tool to confirm it works.</code></pre>

  <p>
    Agents know their own config format better than a settings panel does — and
    on Windows there are more of them to know, since VS Code renamed the object
    and Codex keeps its configuration in TOML. <code>gitwarren agent-setup
    --manual</code> prints all three formats if you would rather edit a file.
  </p>

  <h2>Code inside WSL</h2>

  <p>
    A WSL distribution is a separate machine with its own git, its own paths and
    its own agents. The Windows app installs into it and talks to it over{" "}
    <code>wsl.exe</code>, rather than reading its files through{" "}
    <code>\\wsl$</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    A WSL path offered as an ordinary local repository is refused for that
    reason. <a href="/docs/wsl">WSL, from the Windows app</a> explains what goes
    wrong otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2>git has to be installed</h2>

  <p>
    GitWarren shells out to your own <code>git</code> rather than bundling one,
    so it needs to be on the <code>PATH</code> — Git for Windows is the usual
    answer. If it is missing, the app says so explicitly rather than showing an
    empty list.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <span class="note-title">Every release is tested here</span>
    <p>
      Windows is not a platform this gets ported to afterwards. CI builds and
      runs the test suite on Windows, macOS and Linux on every change, and the
      first run of that pipeline duly found a Windows bug — which is what it is
      for.
    </p>
  </div>
</Docs>
